# Competitor-default product decisions (hard rule)

Operator directive (Phil, 2026-08-16): **when a product decision would
have been asked of Phil, do not ask.** Default to what CyberArk,
Delinea, BeyondTrust, HashiCorp Vault, 1Password Business, and
Bitwarden Enterprise do for that scenario. If no competitor signal,
choose the most responsible, assessor-defensible option for a United
States Government (USG) / enterprise privileged-identity product
(NIST SP 800-53 AC/AU, attribution, least surprise for auditors).
Record the decision and the basis in the commit body. Do not leave
`open-q-*` hanging.

Operator directive (Philip Lieberman, 2026-09-15), verbatim:

> **Our relationships are identical to those of Microsoft and its
> resellers and MSPs.**
>
> **When making a decision, default to Microsoft's position.**

## The rule

> **Never park a product UX, default, or evidence-visibility decision
> on the operator.** Competitor parity first. Else the most
> responsible cybersecurity default. Write the decision and the
> basis into the commit. Close the `open-q-*` in the same change
> set. US English. No lab leaks in UI.

## Which list governs which decision

Two reference lists, two decision classes. They do not compete.

| Decision class | Reference |
| --- | --- |
| Product, feature, operator experience: defaults, column visibility, who owns a secret, what an operator sees | The named privileged-access vendors above (CyberArk, Delinea, BeyondTrust, HashiCorp Vault, 1Password Business, Bitwarden Enterprise) |
| Channel, partner program, licensing structure, reseller and Managed Service Provider (MSP) relationship, seller of record, billing, collection, partner incentive, contract architecture | **Microsoft** and its reseller and MSP relationships |

When a decision spans both, split it: the operator-facing behavior
follows the privileged-access vendors, the commercial and licensing
structure follows Microsoft.

## The invariant Microsoft's model preserves

Microsoft is the channel reference because its model already carries
the shape this product requires.

1. **The licensing relationship runs Provider to Customer, directly
   and always.** The product is United States-controlled
   cryptographic software, so the license cannot be brokered.
2. **A partner may be the commercial seller of record.** A reseller or
   MSP may quote, sell, invoice, and collect funds, and an MSP may
   deliver services in concert with the product.
3. **There is no sublicense arrangement anywhere in the pack.** No
   partner ever holds a license to convey. No partner is ever in the
   licensing chain.

Item 3 is deliberately **contrary to the common software-channel
convention**, in which a reseller takes a license and sublicenses
onward. That is exactly why every resale lane must say so expressly
rather than leave it to inference. A silent agreement will be read
against us by the convention a counterparty's counsel already expects.

## Delegated administration, not a license grant

Where a partner operates, deploys, configures, or administers the
software inside the Customer's environment, its authority comes from
the **Customer's delegation of scoped, revocable administrative
rights** - never from a grant by Provider to the partner. Provider
licenses the Customer; the Customer delegates administration. That
separation is what keeps partner operation from becoming a sublicense.

## Borrowed mechanics stand on their own

Naming Microsoft picks the reference; it does not supply the
justification.

1. **Justify each borrowed mechanic on its own merits** for this
   product's export-control posture and its Cybersecurity Maturity
   Model Certification (CMMC) posture. A counterparty's counsel tests
   the clause, not the comparison.
2. **Never cite the analogy as the authority.** "Microsoft does it
   this way" is a starting point in the commit body, not a defense in
   the clause.
3. **Verify, do not assume.** Microsoft program terms change over
   time. A borrowed mechanic is checked against the current published
   terms before it is relied on.
4. Borrowing a mechanic never claims the product or the Organization
   Seeking Assessment (OSA) is CMMC certified, or that any control is
   Met ([`compliance-claims-honesty.mdc`](compliance-claims-honesty.mdc)).

## Vendor names stay out of the product

Microsoft is a vendor name and obeys the same naming ban as the
privileged-access vendors. Vendor names belong in Cursor rules,
`docs/dev`, and commit bodies only. Never in an operator-facing
website or product surface: no Single-Page Application (SPA) string,
no locale catalog, no Help or manual page, no partner-facing product
copy. See
[`no-competitor-names-in-website.mdc`](no-competitor-names-in-website.mdc).

This rule file is a Cursor rule, not website content, so naming
Microsoft here is correct.

## Required agent behavior

1. **Do not ask** when the alternative is "should we show X?" /
   "what should the default be?" / leaving `open-q-*` for Phil.
2. **Competitor first** - match the named vault / Privileged Access
   Management (PAM) vendors for that scenario (who owns a secret, who
   approved access, what is visible in a list by default).
3. **Else responsible default** - attribution (AU-2/AU-3), least
   privilege, fail closed, auditor-least-surprise. Density or
   chrome compactness is not a reason to hide the accountable
   party.
4. **Record the basis** in the commit body: vendors consulted, or
   the 800-53 / attribution reason when no vendor signal.
5. **Close `open-q-*`** in the same change set. Do not add a new
   parked question.
6. Source identifiers and UI copy stay US English
   ([`enterprise-compliance.mdc`](enterprise-compliance.mdc) section 1).
   Never put lab configuration or agent thinking in operator UI
   ([`no-lab-config-in-operator-ui.mdc`](no-lab-config-in-operator-ui.mdc)).
7. **Website copy never names those vendors.** Write "Competitors".
   Record vendor names in the commit body or `docs/dev` only.
   See [`no-competitor-names-in-website.mdc`](no-competitor-names-in-website.mdc).
8. **Channel decisions record Microsoft as the reference** in the
   commit body, naming the specific mechanic relied on, plus the
   export-control or CMMC reason the mechanic holds here on its own.

## Forbidden

- Asking the operator to pick a default the named competitors
  already settled.
- Asking the operator to pick a channel, licensing-structure, or
  partner-relationship default that Microsoft's model already settles.
- Leaving `open-q-*` in a queue, spec comment, or evidence note
  after the slice that found it.
- Hiding an accountable party (owner, approver, actor) to save
  column width.
- Inventing a fake owner / email-as-identity when the person
  record is missing - bind to the durable principal, or say
  unresolved honestly.
- Any sublicense, license-to-resell, or pass-through license grant to
  a reseller or MSP, in any lane of the contract pack.
- Placing a partner in the licensing chain between Provider and
  Customer, or leaving a resale lane silent so the ordinary
  sublicense convention is read into it.
- Deriving partner administrative authority from a Provider grant
  rather than from the Customer's scoped, revocable delegation.
- Citing "Microsoft does this" as the clause's justification, or
  relying on remembered program terms instead of current ones.
- Naming Microsoft (or any vendor) in a website, SPA, locale catalog,
  Help page, manual, or partner-facing product string.

## Worked example

**Wrong:** Secure Share Owner column exists on the API but starts
hidden; spec comment says `open-q-owner` and the agent asks
whether to show it.

**Right:** CyberArk / Delinea / Bitwarden Enterprise / 1Password
Business show who owns a shared secret. Show Owner by default;
Columns may hide it. Commit body records competitor + AU-2/AU-3.

**Wrong (channel):** A reseller lane is drafted with a license to the
reseller plus a right to sublicense to end customers, because that is
the usual software-channel shape, and the agent asks the operator to
confirm.

**Right (channel):** The reseller is the seller of record and may
invoice and collect. The license is granted by Provider directly to
the Customer, and the lane says expressly that no sublicense is
granted and that the reseller holds no license to convey. Where the
partner administers the deployment, the Customer delegates scoped,
revocable administrative rights. Commit body records Microsoft as the
channel reference, the seller-of-record and delegated-administration
mechanics relied on, and the export-control reason the direct license
is required here.

